

#----To prepare for software development roles and technical interviews, focus on the core Operating Systems (OS) concepts that directly impact software design, performance, and system behavior:-------

1. Process & Thread Management
Processes vs. Threads: Differences, execution models, and memory layouts (Heap, Stack, Code, Data).

Context Switching: How CPU switches execution contexts, overhead involved, and state preservation.

Concurrency & Multithreading: Thread lifecycles, user-level vs. kernel-level threads, and multithreading models.

CPU Scheduling: Scheduling algorithms (FCFS, SJF, Round Robin, Priority Scheduling, Multi-level Queue).



cpu scheduling scheduling algo fcfs sjf round robin 

2. Synchronization & Deadlocks
Race Conditions & Critical Section: Identification of concurrency bugs and critical sections.

Synchronization Primitives: Mutexes, Semaphores, Spinlocks, Condition Variables, and Monitors.

Deadlocks:

Four necessary conditions (Mutual Exclusion, Hold and Wait, No Preemption, Circular Wait).

Detection, Prevention, Avoidance (Banker’s Algorithm), and Recovery techniques

3. Memory Management
Virtual Memory: Pages, frames, page tables, and Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB).

Memory Allocation: Paging, Segmentation, and memory fragmentation (Internal vs. External).

Page Faults & Swapping: Page replacement algorithms (FIFO, LRU, LFU, Optimal).

Stack vs. Heap: Dynamic vs. static allocation, cache locality, and garbage collection principles.

4. Storage & File Systems
File Operations: File descriptors, file allocation methods (Contiguous, Linked, Indexed), and inodes.

Disk Management: Disk scheduling algorithms (SSTF, SCAN, C-SCAN) and I/O buffering.

5. System Calls & I/O Systems
Kernel vs. User Mode: System call execution flow, dual-mode operation, and interrupts/traps.

I/O Models: Synchronous vs. Asynchronous I/O, Blocking vs. Non-blocking I/O. 


